    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `publish_run` in `app/publish.py`, and both tests pass (the existing one and a new one I added). Nothing has been uploaded to the real Hub; the tests use a fake API object.\n\n**Which upload function it uses:** huggingface_hub 2.0.0 no longer has `upload_large_folder`, and neither `HfApi` nor the package offers another function under that name. In this version, the ordinary `HfApi.upload_folder` handles very large folders, as long as `hf_xet` is installed (it is, at 1.6.0). According to its documentation, it then:\n- splits a large folder into several commits to stay under the Hub's limits, so the number of files doesn't make it fail;\n- if an upload is interrupted, calling it again resumes it: files already committed are skipped and data already uploaded isn't sent again.\n\n**What `publish_run` does:**\n1. Creates the dataset repo as private, or uses it if it already exists.\n2. Uploads `run_dir` to the root of the repo with the same folder layout, skipping `*.tmp` files at any depth and everything under the top-level `wandb/` folder. A `wandb/` folder nested deeper would still be uploaded; tell me if those should be skipped too.\n\nI added an optional `api` argument, the same way `publish_summary` takes one, so tests can pass in a fake. The new test checks the calls it makes and runs the skip patterns through the library's own file filter.\n\n**One thing to watch:** if `hf_xet` is ever missing, `upload_folder` goes back to a single commit. Then very large runs could fail and wouldn't resume. It's a dependency of `huggingface-hub` and is installed now, but you may want to make sure it's in your production image."
